Abstract = {Soil quality and plant diversity are essential for the health and
   functioning of urban green spaces (UGS). This study evaluated the soil
   quality of four UGS types in Hangzhou city using an enhanced Nemerow
   index based on four physical and seven fertility indicators, and
   quantified plant diversity in arbor, shrub, and herb layers using four
   diversity indices across UGS types. Relationships between soil
   conditions and plant diversity were also examined. Results indicated an
   acceptable overall soil quality (P-N = 1.07) of the UGS types, but
   individual indicators were highly unbalanced. Soil electrical
   conductivity and clay content were relatively high (grade I-III),
   whereas pH and bulk density spanned grade I-VI. Except for soil organic
   matter, available P and available K, most fertility indicators (TN, TP,
   TK and AN) were severely deficient (average grade V), with TN and TP as
   the main limiting factors. Scenic recreational green spaces (SRGS) had
   the best soil quality, while transportation green spaces (TGS) ranked
   lowest, below residential (RGS) and park green spaces (PGS). Plant
   diversity was highest in the arbor layer (S = 7.18) and lowest in the
   shrub layer (S = 1.79), giving the order arbor layer > herb layer >
   shrub layer. Among USG types, SRGS had the highest plant diversity,
   followed by RGS, PGS and TGS. In PGS, 69.42\% of soil-plant diversity
   correlations were positive, indicating that balanced nutrients and
   suitable bulk density favor higher diversity. In contrast, 46.28\%,
   52.07\%, and 52.89\% of correlations in RGS, TGS, and SRGS were
   negative, associated with excess available nutrients, high bulk density,
   and strong interspecific competition. Overall, UGS health depends on the
   balance between soil nutrients, physical structure, and plant diversity,
   with management intensity mediating these relationships.},
